    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The honest answer is that you cannot tell by standing in your basement and sniffing. Human noses adapt to a smell within minutes, and the damp that matters is measured against a number, not a feeling. Buy a hygrometer before you buy anything else.
        </p>

        <div id="three-day-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Three-Day Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy two inexpensive digital hygrometers. They cost about ten dollars each and they will tell you more than any amount of reading. Place one in the middle of the open basement floor and one in the dampest-feeling spot you have, which is usually a corner against an exterior wall or behind stored boxes. Leave them for three days, and pick three days in the humid part of the year, because a basement tested in a dry March tells you nothing about July.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read them morning and evening and note the highest figure each shows. What you are looking for is the sustained peak, not the average, because mold responds to the conditions it gets rather than the conditions on average. Three days is enough to catch a genuine pattern and short enough that people actually do it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sustained Peak Reading</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 50%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Genuinely dry basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No dehumidifier needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50-55%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine, watch it in a wet summer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recheck after heavy rain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">55-60%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Borderline, smell will appear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A dehumidifier is worthwhile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60-70%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mold-supporting, mites active</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, plus find the source</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Above 70%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Active moisture source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diagnose before buying</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The gap between your two hygrometers matters as much as the readings themselves. A basement whose open floor reads 52 and whose far corner reads 68 does not have a mild problem, it has a serious problem in one location and an air circulation problem on top of it. Mold does not care about the room average. It grows where the surface humidity is highest, which is why it appears behind boxes and along the base of the coldest wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            One refinement worth the extra five minutes. Test after three consecutive days of rain, not during a dry spell. A basement that reads 54 percent in settled weather and 71 percent four days after a storm is telling you the moisture is coming through the ground, which is a different problem from summer air infiltration and needs a different fix.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE THRESHOLD</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">60 percent sustained is the line.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Below 50, you do not need a dehumidifier. Between 50 and 60, one will make the space more pleasant and protect what you store there. Above 60 sustained, common indoor molds can establish and dust mites reproduce freely, and the question stops being optional.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="signals-yes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Signals That Mean Yes Before You Even Measure
          </h2>

          <p>
            The hygrometer is the arbiter, but some signs are conclusive enough on their own that you can order the machine while you wait for the instruments to arrive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Musty Smell</h3>

          <p>
            That specific basement smell is microbial volatile organic compounds, produced by mold and bacteria as they metabolise. It is not dust, it is not old concrete, and it is not the smell of a basement. It is biological activity, and it means something has had surface humidity around 60 percent for long enough to establish. Crucially, you will stop noticing it within about ten minutes of going down, so judge it in the first thirty seconds after opening the door, or ask someone who does not live there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensation on Cold Pipes or Walls</h3>

          <p>
            Beads of water on a cold water pipe, on the outside of a toilet tank, or on the lower foundation wall in summer are unambiguous. That water came out of the air, which means the air has more moisture in it than the cold surfaces can tolerate. No further diagnosis is needed to conclude that the air is too humid, though you may still need to find out why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rust on Tools and Cast Iron</h3>

          <p>
            Surface rust appearing on hand tools, table saw tops, or the cast iron of a bench vise is a reliable and slightly earlier indicator than mold. Steel starts corroding meaningfully above about 60 percent relative humidity, and it does so without any visible water. If your tools are furring up in the basement, the air is above the threshold whether or not anything smells yet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Doors and Drawers That Stick in Summer</h3>

          <p>
            Wood absorbs moisture from the air and swells. A basement door that closes easily in February and binds in July is a moisture gauge you already own. The same goes for a workbench drawer, or a wooden stair tread that creaks differently in summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Damp or Cool-to-the-Touch Cardboard</h3>

          <p>
            Cardboard boxes stored against a foundation wall go soft and slightly cool to the touch well before they look damaged. Pull one away from the wall and feel the face that was against it. If it is limp, the air behind it has been sitting above 65 percent for a long time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="condensation-seepage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Condensation or Seepage: The Test That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most useful diagnostic in the whole subject, because it separates a problem a dehumidifier solves from a problem it only hides. Damp on a basement wall comes from one of two directions: out of the air onto the wall, or through the wall from the soil. They look identical and they need completely different responses.
          </p>

          <p>
            The test is a square of aluminium foil or clear plastic sheet, about a foot on each side, taped down on all four edges to a bare section of the wall or floor where the damp appears. Leave it for two or three days in humid weather, then pull it off and look at which side is wet.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Result</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Diagnosis</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Fixes It</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moisture on the room-facing side</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Condensation from the air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A dehumidifier</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moisture on the wall-facing side</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seepage through the masonry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drainage, grading, sealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Both sides wet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both problems at once</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix the seepage first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Neither side wet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not an active moisture path</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Look elsewhere for the source</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the water is on the room side, the air is depositing moisture on a cold surface and a dehumidifier is exactly the right tool. If the water is on the wall side, moisture is being driven through the masonry from the soil, and a dehumidifier will evaporate it as fast as it arrives while the wall stays permanently wet inside. That means efflorescence, spalling, and eventually structural deterioration, all while the room reads a comfortable 50 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            That second case is why this page is not simply a recommendation to buy a dehumidifier. A machine masking active seepage is worse than no machine, because it removes the symptom that would otherwise have prompted the repair. Our guides on how to fix a damp basement and wet basement solutions cover what the repair actually involves and what it costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both sides wet is the most common result in an older house, and it means you have genuine seepage and a humid room. Deal with the seepage, then retest the air. Very often the humidity problem substantially resolves itself once the wall stops contributing moisture, and you need a smaller machine than you thought.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What a Dehumidifier Genuinely Fixes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Held at 50 percent, a correctly sized and drained dehumidifier reliably solves a specific and worthwhile list of problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            It stops the musty smell, though not immediately. The microbial compounds responsible are absorbed into porous materials and outgas for weeks after the air is dry, so expect the smell to fade over two to six weeks rather than vanish overnight. If it has not substantially gone after six weeks at 50 percent, there is a colonised material down there that needs removing rather than drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            It stops condensation on cold surfaces, which is the most immediately visible win. Pipes stop dripping, the wall stops beading, and whatever was stored under the drip stops getting wet. It protects stored goods: cardboard stays rigid, paper and photographs stop cockling, fabric stops going musty, and tools stop rusting. For anyone using a basement as a workshop, the rust alone justifies the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            It makes a finished basement genuinely more comfortable, and in summer it makes it feel cooler even though a dehumidifier adds heat to the room. Drier air lets sweat evaporate, so the same temperature feels several degrees cooler. And it substantially reduces dust mite populations, which matters if the space is used as a bedroom or a playroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also prevents new mold from establishing, which is worth stating precisely. Sustained humidity below 50 percent stops germination and stops existing growth spreading. It does not kill or remove what is already there, and dead mold is still an allergen. Existing visible growth has to be physically cleaned off hard surfaces, and porous materials that are colonised have to be thrown away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-doesnt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What It Will Not Fix, However Long You Run It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Being clear about the limits is what stops people buying a second, larger machine when the first one did not solve the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            It will not stop water entering through a crack, a cove joint, or a failed window well. A dehumidifier removes water vapour from air; it has no effect whatsoever on liquid water arriving under pressure. If your floor is wet after rain, that is a drainage and repair problem, and running a dehumidifier against it is like mopping with the tap on.
          </p>

          <p>
            It will not remove existing mold. It stops it growing, which is essential, but the visible growth is still there and still allergenic. Hard surfaces need physically cleaning; colonised drywall, carpet, insulation and cardboard need removing. A dehumidifier is the thing that stops it coming back after you have done that work.
          </p>

          <p>
            It will not fix a basement whose humidity is coming from outdoor air through a large opening. If a bulkhead door, an unsealed rim joist, or an open window is letting warm humid summer air into a cool basement, the machine is effectively dehumidifying the outdoors and will run continuously and lose. Air sealing the rim joist is often the highest-value single action in the entire project, and it frequently drops the required machine capacity by a whole tier.
          </p>

          <p>
            And it will not usefully dry a cold basement in midwinter. Below roughly 60 degrees a compressor unit begins frosting its coil and spends a growing share of its runtime defrosting rather than drying. In most climates that does not matter because winter basements are naturally dry, but if yours is damp in January you need a desiccant unit or a different diagnosis, which our winter dehumidifier guide covers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Changed in Our Test Basement at 50% RH</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 wks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Until musty smell largely cleared</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">New rust on stored tools</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">None</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Effect on the post-rain floor seep</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Unchanged</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Existing wall mold, until cleaned</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="genuinely-no" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Cases Where the Answer Is Genuinely No
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plenty of advice assumes every basement needs a dehumidifier. Several common situations do not, and buying one anyway means paying for electricity and shortening a machine's life for nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A basement that reads below 50 percent through a humid August does not need one, and that is more common than people expect in newer houses with conditioned basements, good exterior drainage, and a sealed rim joist. If your test comes back at 47 percent in July, the correct action is to do nothing and retest next year.
          </p>

          <p>
            A basement that is fully conditioned as part of the house, with supply and return registers on the central system, is often held dry enough by the air conditioning alone during the cooling season. Air conditioning is a dehumidifier that happens to also cool. The failure case is a mild, humid shoulder season when the air conditioning barely runs, so test in June and September rather than in the peak.
          </p>

          <p>
            A basement in a genuinely dry climate rarely needs one. In much of the mountain west and southwest, outdoor dew points stay low enough through the year that basement humidity is a non-issue, and the local problem is more often air that is too dry. Regional advice written for the humid east does not transfer.
          </p>

          <p>
            And a basement whose damp is entirely seepage does not need one yet. That is not a permanent no, it is a not-first: fix the water path, then retest. Buying the machine first is how a repairable crack becomes a decade of masked deterioration.
          </p>

          <p>
            One genuine alternative deserves mention. If your basement is only marginally damp and the moisture is coming from inside the house rather than the ground, improved ventilation can be enough on its own. Venting a dryer properly, adding a bathroom extractor, or running a small continuous-exhaust fan can move a borderline basement below the threshold without a dehumidifier at all. Our guide on keeping a basement dry without a dehumidifier covers those routes in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Order of Operations Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the test says yes, there is a sequence that saves money and produces a better result than starting with the purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Fix the Outside</h3>

          <p>
            Extend downspouts at least four to six feet from the foundation, and check that the ground slopes away from the house rather than toward it. This is free or nearly free, it addresses the largest single source of basement moisture in most houses, and it is genuinely common for it to solve a damp basement outright. Do it before anything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Seal the Air Leaks</h3>

          <p>
            The rim joist, the band around the top of the foundation wall where the floor framing sits, is the biggest uncontrolled opening in most basements. Sealing and insulating it stops warm humid summer air pouring in, and it typically drops the dehumidifier capacity you need by a full tier. Weatherstrip a bulkhead door while you are at it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Deal With Internal Sources</h3>

          <p>
            Check that the clothes dryer vents outside and that its duct is actually connected, because a disconnected dryer duct can add several pints a day on its own. Cover a sump pit with a sealed lid. Insulate cold water pipes so they stop condensing. Each is cheap and each removes load permanently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Then Size and Buy</h3>

          <p>
            Only now decide the capacity, because the first three steps will have changed the answer. Size from the floor area, correct for an honest dampness grade, and check where the water will drain. Our sizing guide walks through that calculation with worked examples, and our tested roundup covers which machines actually deliver their rating at basement temperatures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Basement by Smell Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your nose adapts to a smell within about ten minutes, so by the time you have walked around assessing the space you can no longer detect what you came to assess. Judge in the first thirty seconds after opening the door, ask a visitor, or better, use a ten-dollar hygrometer and a number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing in the Wrong Season</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A basement tested in March tells you almost nothing about July. Summer is when warm humid outdoor air meets cool basement surfaces, which is the condition that produces the problem. Test over three days in the humid part of the year, and ideally after three consecutive days of rain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Dehumidifier to Manage Active Seepage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A machine that evaporates seepage as fast as it arrives keeps the room at a comfortable 50 percent while the wall stays permanently wet inside, which produces efflorescence, spalling and eventual deterioration with no visible symptom to prompt the repair. Run the foil test first and fix the water path.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Free Fixes and Starting With the Purchase</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extending downspouts, grading soil away from the house, and sealing the rim joist cost little or nothing and frequently drop a basement below the threshold on their own. Done first, they also reduce the capacity you need to buy. Done after, you have already overspent on a machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Remove Mold That Is Already There</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sustained humidity below 50 percent stops mold germinating and stops existing growth spreading, which is exactly what you want, but it neither kills nor removes what has already grown, and dead mold is still an allergen. Clean hard surfaces physically and discard colonised porous materials, then run the dehumidifier to keep it from returning.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does every basement need a dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A basement that stays below 50 percent relative humidity through a humid August does not need one, which is reasonably common in newer houses with a sealed rim joist, good exterior drainage and a conditioned basement. It is also unnecessary in genuinely dry climates. Measure over three summer days before assuming.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my basement is too humid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Put two cheap digital hygrometers in the basement, one in the open and one in the dampest corner, and read them over three humid days. A sustained peak above 60 percent means mold can establish and dust mites reproduce. A large gap between the two readings means you have a circulation problem as well as a humidity one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a musty basement smell always a humidity problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is always a biological activity problem, and biological activity needs moisture, so effectively yes. The smell is microbial volatile organic compounds from mold and bacteria, which need surface humidity around 60 percent to stay active. What it does not tell you is whether that moisture comes from the air or through the wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier stop water coming through my basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and this is the most important limitation to understand. A dehumidifier removes water vapour from air and has no effect on liquid water arriving under hydrostatic pressure. Water appearing on the floor after rain is a drainage and repair problem; running a dehumidifier against it masks the symptom while the cause continues.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can I tell condensation from seepage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tape a one-foot square of aluminium foil or clear plastic to the bare wall or floor where the damp appears, seal all four edges, and leave it two to three days in humid weather. Moisture on the room-facing side is condensation, which a dehumidifier fixes. Moisture on the wall-facing side is seepage, which it does not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can air conditioning replace a basement dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. Air conditioning dehumidifies as a side effect of cooling, so a basement fully integrated into the central system is often dry enough during the cooling season. The failure case is a mild humid shoulder season when the system barely runs, which is exactly when a cool basement is most prone to condensation. Test in June and September.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before a dehumidifier gets rid of the smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two to six weeks at a sustained 50 percent, not overnight. The compounds responsible are absorbed into porous materials and outgas slowly after the air is dry. If the smell has not substantially gone after six weeks at target humidity, there is a colonised material down there that needs removing rather than drying out.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

