        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Spend ten dollars on a hygrometer before you spend two hundred on a machine. Three days of readings in humid weather, one instrument in the open and one in the dampest corner, answers the question better than any checklist. Below 50 percent sustained, do nothing. Between 50 and 60, a dehumidifier is a worthwhile improvement. Above 60 sustained, it is not optional.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then run the foil test before you buy, because it separates the problem a dehumidifier solves from the one it only hides. Condensation on the room side means the machine is the right answer. Moisture on the wall side means water is coming through the masonry, and the repair comes first. A dehumidifier managing a solved problem lasts years; one fighting an unsolved one does not.
          </p>
        </div>

